        <mxCell id="2nj_4GHoPnptEzptErEp-52" parent="1" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;rounded=1;fontSize=25;" value="&lt;font&gt;In a phishing attack, threat actors typically only gain access to the resources the compromised user is authorized to use—unless they perform &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;3,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;143&quot; style=&quot;&quot;&gt;privilege escalation&lt;/b&gt;. However, if the &lt;b data-path-to-node=&quot;3,0&quot; data-index-in-node=&quot;181&quot; style=&quot;&quot;&gt;system&lt;/b&gt; itself is the target and is successfully breached, the attacker can gain full control, allowing them to manipulate the resource and impact all users who rely on it.&lt;/font&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="240" width="1423" x="1456" y="9596" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
